#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ring-screens-test — runs the lock screen, the login page and the boot
splash live, drives them through their states, and measures the frames.

The three screens share one design (config/hypr/hyprlock.conf is the
reference; system/usr/local/bin/monogreet and system/usr/share/plymouth/
themes/mono follow it) and one unit, the panel's own pixel. This tool
checks the claim with numbers rather than eyes: ring diameter, edge,
centre, the hour's and the line's ink, the field's opacity through its
fade, and the words the lock and the login page say for caps lock and
a wrong password, on each screen, at the scale the panel runs; on the
splash, which has no ring, the prompt's and the dots' place. Every run
writes its frames and a report into a fresh directory and leaves them
there.

  ring-screens-test lock          the lock screen (hyprlock)
  ring-screens-test login         the login page (monogreet)
  ring-screens-test splash        the boot splash (plymouthd)
  ring-screens-test all
      --scale S     compositor scale for the nested run (default: the
                    host's, so the numbers are the panel's)
      --out DIR     where the run goes (default: /tmp/ring-screens-test/<stamp>)
      --keep-going  run every check even after one fails

How each screen runs:

lock, login — a nested niri (its winit window, fullscreened on the host
by window id so the nested output is the panel's size) at the chosen
scale. hyprlock or monogreet runs inside it; keys go in through wtype
(zwp_virtual_keyboard_v1 on the nested seat — no host focus is needed),
frames come out through grim on the nested socket. The nested
compositor only repaints while the host presents its window, so the
host must be unlocked and the window visible: the tool refuses to run
on a locked session and fails the run if the frames stop moving. The
lock runs on a copy of the config that sends PAM to the `vlock`
service (auth required pam_unix.so, nothing else): it asks for the
password as the real service does, a wrong try reaches no faillock
counter, and it is answered after pam_unix's fail delay (about two
seconds) — so the lock's "checking" is visible here, and the frames
after Return are read against that delay. (PAM's `other` service,
pam_deny, fails before asking anything; hyprlock 0.9.6 then restarts
the conversation at once, forever, and never paints a frame.)
monogreet talks to a fake greetd served by this tool (a secret prompt,
then auth_error after a pause).

splash — plymouthd in a user namespace (it insists on uid 0 and on a
tty: unshare -Urm and script(1) give it both), the repo's theme bound
over the installed one, label-pango hidden and the fonts directory
replaced by what the initramfs packs (Plymouth.ttf and friends), so
label-freetype renders as it does at boot. plymouthd picks the x11
renderer when DISPLAY is set: a 1920x1080 X window on the desktop, which
reads X input only, so its keys go in through XTEST and its frames come
from grim on the host — the one screen that needs the host's attention
for its few seconds.

Needs: niri, grim, wtype, imagemagick, plymouth, libxtst; the host
unlocked; and for the splash, DISPLAY (xwayland-satellite).
"""

